// repro-30 — `<match for=P on=@cell.subfield>` ignores the `.subfield` member access
//   and dispatches on the WHOLE cell object, so no variant arm ever matches → the
//   match mount renders blank (not even the initial/seed variant). Compile exit-0.
//
// Compiler: ../scrml @ 7c01b22a (pkg v0.7.0). Command: scrml compile repro-30.scrml -o out
//
// Expected: the match dispatch reads `@cell.state` (an enum variant) and renders the
//   matching arm — here the .Idle seed should paint "IDLE".
// Actual:   the emitted dispatch is `__scrml_match_..._dispatch(_scrml_reactive_get("cell"))`
//   — it passes the whole `{ state, n }` object, never `.state`. The dispatcher's
//   `_tag = (_v.variant) ? _v.variant : _v` then gets the object, matches no arm, and
//   the mount stays empty. (Identical emit to `on=@cell`, i.e. the `.state` path is dropped.)
//
// giti impact: live.scrml (`on=@snapshot.state`) and feed.scrml (`on=@status.state`)
//   render a blank State — the channel/SSE cells carry extra fields (changed,
//   conflicts, …) so they must be a struct with an enum `state` subfield, which this
//   bug makes unrenderable. (feed additionally crashes — see the SSE seed-clobber in
//   the 2026-06-22 three-codegen-findings report, finding #2.)

<program>

type P:enum = { Idle  Ok }

<cell> = { state: P.Idle, n: 0 }

<div>
  <match for=P on=@cell.state>
    <Idle><p>IDLE</p></Idle>
    <Ok><p>OK</p></Ok>
  </match>
</div>

</program>
